<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zirid dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Zirid</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Zirid dynasty was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanhaja">Sanhaja</a> Berber dynasty that governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a>, roughly present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, on behalf of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a> after the Fatimids moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. The Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a> profile records that between about 1048 and 1052 the Zirid governor abandoned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Ismaili</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiism">Shi'ism</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> orthodoxy. This defection severed the caliphate's North African domain and was an early stage in Fatimid decline.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zirid-dynasty.jpg" alt="Zirid dynasty" width="320" height="184" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: R Prazeres (using file uploaded by Kabyle20 as template) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zirid_control_circa_980_(version_2).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
